course: dictionary
lesson: 4130
title: Glint: a flash of light or a look in the eye
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A glint is a small, quick flash of light. You can see it when light bounces off something shiny, like water, glass or metal. «Far ahead they saw a glint of the familiar old river.» The river did not glow all over. It flashed for a moment, so glint must mean a quick flash of light.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Far ahead» tells you they were looking from a distance, and «saw» tells you it was something they could see. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Far ahead they saw a flash of the familiar old river» still makes sense, so you have worked it out.
explain: Glint can also be a look in someone's eye. «A glint in her eye» shows a feeling such as anger, fun or a clever plan. Check the clues: a glint that is in an eye is about feeling, not light. In a story, «a knife glints in the dark» tells you the knife flashes, and «a villain's eyes glint» tells you the villain looks cold or sly.
fact: Glint can be a verb (to glint, it glints, it glinted) or a noun (a glint, glints).
fact: «Glint» as a flash means a short, bright reflection of light.
fact: «Glint» in the eye means a look that shows a feeling, such as anger, fun or a clever plan.
fact: Glints of light often come from shiny surfaces like water, glass and metal.
fact: Good writers use glints to build tension: «a knife glints in the dark» makes the reader feel that danger is close.
exercise: «Far ahead they saw a glint of the familiar old river.» What does glint mean here? | A long, slow walk | A deep, dark hole in the ground | *A small, bright flash of light | A loud sound from far away
exercise: «A knife glints in the dark.» What is glinting? | *Something shiny that flashes light | A soft, warm blanket | A quiet animal | The night sky
exercise: In «a glint in her eye», what does glint show? | The colour of her eyes | She needs new glasses | *A feeling such as anger, fun or a clever plan | She is fast asleep
test: «The sun glinted off the lake.» In this sentence, what kind of word is glinted? | A noun | *A verb | An adjective | A full stop
test: «Far ahead they saw a glint of the familiar old river.» Which swap still makes sense? | A hill of | A tune of | A sock of | *A flash of
exam: «The villain's eyes glint in the dark.» What does glint suggest about the villain? | A cosy, sleepy feeling | Going to bed early | A sound of thunder | *A cold, sly or menacing look
